\details{
The input can be either a \code{.gpkg} file created by
\code{\link[=oe_vectortranslate]{oe_vectortranslate()}} or a \code{.osm.pbf} file. In the second case, the
function runs \code{\link[=oe_vectortranslate]{oe_vectortranslate()}} first (using the arguments
\code{extra_tags}, \code{boundary}, and \code{boundary_type}) and exports the resulting
\code{.gpkg} file. DuckDB does not read \code{.osm.pbf} files directly because its
GDAL OSM driver can silently drop features when it reads a single layer
from a large file, so GDAL is always used for the \code{.osm.pbf} to \code{.gpkg}
step.

The output files are created in the same directory as the input file:
\itemize{
\item \code{format = "parquet"} creates one file for each layer, named
\verb{<input name>_<layer>.parquet};
\item \code{format = "duckdb"} creates a single \verb{<input name>.duckdb} file with one
table for each layer.
}

The geometry column keeps its name and CRS. Parquet files are compressed
with zstd. The export is skipped if the output already exists, unless
\code{force_export = TRUE}. It is never skipped when \code{extra_tags} or \code{boundary}
are not \code{NULL}.

This function requires the suggested packages \code{duckdb} and \code{DBI}. The
first time it runs, DuckDB downloads its spatial extension, so an internet
connection is needed.
\subsection{Experimental: \code{engine = "osmium"}}{

With \code{engine = "osmium"} the \code{.osm.pbf} file is read directly by DuckDB
using the \href{https://duckdb.org/community_extensions/extensions/osmium}{osmium}
community extension (which builds geometries with libosmium), so no
\code{.gpkg} file is created. On a state-sized extract (Maryland, 216 MB) it was
about 3 times faster than GDAL, but it needed much more memory: about 1.1
to 1.5 GB for one layer and up to 2.4 GB for three layers in a single
call, against less than 0.5 GB for GDAL. Exporting one layer at a time
reduces the peak memory. The extension must be available for the DuckDB
version used by the \code{duckdb} package (a local, unsigned build is also
accepted).

The layers are defined with SQL rules that reproduce those in
\code{osmconf.ini}. In our tests the \code{points} and \code{lines} layers contained the
same features as the ones created by GDAL, while \code{multipolygons} differed
in about 0.06\% of the features. Other differences are:
\itemize{
\item only the layers \code{points}, \code{lines} and \code{multipolygons} are available;
\item the output has the columns \code{type} (\code{node}, \code{way} or \code{relation}), \code{id},
\code{geometry}, and \code{tags}, a \code{MAP} column with \emph{all} the tags (instead of
one column for each tag and \code{other_tags}). The argument \code{extra_tags} is
ignored;
\item simple polygons are \code{POLYGON}s instead of \code{MULTIPOLYGON}s;
\item features whose geometry could not be built are dropped, and the
function reports how many;
\item \code{boundary} is applied after reading the file (so it does not speed up the
process), keeping the features that intersect the boundary (or its
bounding box if \code{boundary_type = "spat"}). \code{"clipdst"} is treated as
\code{"clipsrc"} since the output always uses EPSG:4326;
\item the output files are named \verb{<input name>_osmium_<layer>.parquet} or
\verb{<input name>_osmium.duckdb}.
